tel 1.0

base tarantula
import soundness

info   browser automation over the W3C WebDriver protocol: sessions, elements, actions and screenshots
detail
    _Tarantula_ drives a web browser from Scala through the
    [WebDriver](https://www.w3.org/TR/webdriver2/) protocol. A browser is a value — `WebDriver.Chrome`,
    `WebDriver.Firefox`, `WebDriver.Safari` or `WebDriver.Edge`, each the standard driver program for
    its browser — and opening a session on it launches the driver, waits for it to listen, creates a
    session and, when the block ends, deletes the session and stops the driver, whether the block
    returned or threw. A driver that is already running, or a remote Selenium grid, is driven through
    the same `WebDriver.Session` interface with `WebDriver(url, capabilities)`.

    Within the session, `browser` is the handle in scope: it navigates, reads the title and source,
    finds elements by typed criteria — an HTML tag, a validated DOM id, a class list, a CSS selector
    list, an XPath or link text, anything with a `Focusable` instance — switches windows and frames,
    handles alerts, manages cookies and timeouts, performs keyboard and pointer actions, runs
    JavaScript, prints the page and takes screenshots.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import probates.awaitProbate
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import threads.virtualThreads
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
        browser.title()
    ```

    A session needs internet access (`internetAccess.online`), an HTTP client to speak to the driver
    (`httpBackends.javaNetHttp`), a `Tactic[WebDriver.Error]` and `Diagnostics`, and a logger for
    `Http.Event`s; launching a local driver additionally needs a `Monitor` (a `supervise` block), a
    `WorkingDirectory`, an `Environment` for the driver process and a logger for `Exec.Event`s.

    The `WebDriver.Session` handle is a capability, confined by capture checking to the block that
    owns it: returning it, or anything that captures it, is a compile error. `WebDriver.Element`
    handles are pure values, but every operation on one takes the session as a contextual parameter,
    so a handle is inert outside its session, and one used after its page has changed raises
    `WebDriver.Error` with reason `StaleElement`. Every failure — a driver that cannot be reached, a
    reply of the wrong shape, or any error the driver reports — arrives as one `WebDriver.Error`,
    carrying the W3C error code as its `WebDriver.Error.Reason`.

    Element selection is spelled `\`, not `/`: `browser \ Li`, `element \ cls"item"` and
    `shadowRoot \ Button` each return every match, and `element(…)` exactly one, raising
    `NoSuchElement` otherwise. The backslash leaves symbolism's arithmetic `/` free to divide
    quantities in the same scope.

    The library has two source sets:

    - `tarantula.core` — `WebDriver` and its nested `Session`, `Element`, `ShadowRoot`, `Error` and
      `Local`, the `browser` accessor and the element extensions (`click`, `value`, `innerText`,
      `attribute`, …). It needs a driver binary (`chromedriver`, `geckodriver`, `safaridriver` or
      `msedgedriver`) on the path to launch a browser; the protocol layer itself is plain HTTP and
      can be exercised against any WebDriver endpoint.
    - `tarantula.image` — the `screenshot()` extensions, which decode a screenshot into a
      `Raster in Png` through hallucination, kept apart so that browser automation does not depend
      on an image codec.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, so a user writes `import soundness.*` and
    imports the ambient givens above by name.

refer tarantula.WebDriver
refer tarantula.browser
refer anticipation.Focusable

entry .WebDriver   a browser to drive: a local driver program, or a driver already listening at a URL
  detail
      `WebDriver` is two things which share a name. As a **type**, it is a driver that is already
      listening — one started by hand, or a Selenium grid — addressed by its `#base` URL and the
      `#capabilities` it will be asked for, and made with `WebDriver(url"http://localhost:4444",
      capabilities)`. As an **object**, it is the home of the four local browsers (`.Chrome`,
      `.Firefox`, `.Safari`, `.Edge`), each a `WebDriver.Local` which launches its driver program for
      the duration of a session, and of the types every session uses: `Session`, `Element`,
      `ShadowRoot` and `Error`.

      Both are `Sessional`, so `driver.session: session ?=> …` opens a session on either, and the
      session is closed — and a launched driver stopped — in a `finally` when the block ends.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      supervise:
        WebDriver.Firefox.headless.on(4445).session:
          browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
          browser.element(H1).innerText()

      WebDriver(url"http://localhost:4444", WebDriver.Chrome.capabilities).session:
        browser.title()
      ```

      A `WebDriver` built with `apply` sends its `capabilities` JSON as the `POST /session` payload
      verbatim, so a Selenium grid's own extensions, proxies, timeouts and mobile emulation can all
      be requested through it.
  refer tarantula.WebDriver.Session
  refer tarantula.WebDriver.Local
  refer tarantula.WebDriver.Error
  refer tarantula.browser
  entry .Driver   the four W3C driver programs: chromedriver, geckodriver, safaridriver and msedgedriver
    detail
        An enumeration of the driver programs a `WebDriver.Local` can launch, as data rather than
        subclasses: `Chromedriver`, `Geckodriver`, `Safaridriver` and `Edgedriver`. Each knows the
        command which starts it on a port, the `browserName` capability its browser answers to, and
        the arguments which put that browser in headless mode. Adding a browser is a new case, since
        the process lifecycle is the same for all of them.
    entry #command   the shell command which starts the driver listening on a port
        `chromedriver --port=N` and `msedgedriver --port=N` take the port with an equals sign;
        `geckodriver --port N` and `safaridriver --port N` take it as a separate argument. The
        driver binary must be on the path.
    entry #browserName   the `browserName` capability the driver's browser answers to
        `chrome`, `firefox`, `safari` or `MicrosoftEdge`, as the W3C capabilities object spells
        them.
    entry #headless   the browser arguments which select headless mode, if the browser has one
        `--headless=new` for Chrome and Edge (the supported spelling since the old `--headless`
        became its alias), the single-dashed `-headless` for Firefox, and nothing for Safari, which
        has no headless mode: asking for one is a no-op rather than an error.
  entry .Local   a browser and the driver program which drives it, as a pure value
    detail
        A `WebDriver.Local` names a browser to drive by its `WebDriver.Driver`, the port the driver
        should listen on (`4444` unless `#on` says otherwise), the arguments the browser is launched
        with and, optionally, a capabilities object to send in place of the generated one. It holds
        no process, connection or session id, so it may be a `val` shared between tests and used for
        several sessions in turn; `.Chrome`, `.Firefox`, `.Safari` and `.Edge` are the four standard
        values.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        val chrome: WebDriver.Local = WebDriver.Chrome.headless.on(4450).arguing(t"--window-size=1280,800")

        supervise:
          chrome.session:
            browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
            browser.title()
        ```

        Opening a session forks the driver's command, polls its `GET /status` endpoint every 50
        milliseconds for up to five seconds until it answers, opens the session with
        `#capabilities`, and stops the driver when the block ends. A driver that cannot be forked
        raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `SessionNotCreated`, and one that never starts listening
        raises one with reason `Timeout`. Two sessions on the same port at once will collide, so a
        test suite running sessions in parallel gives each its own with `#on`.
    refer tarantula.WebDriver.Driver
    refer tarantula.WebDriver.Session
    entry .Sessional   opens a session on a `WebDriver.Local`: forks the driver, waits, drives, stops
      detail
          The `aperture.Sessional` instance for `WebDriver.Local`, with `Self = Local` and `Result =
          Session^`. It needs an `Online`, an `Http.Backend`, a `WorkingDirectory`, an `Environment`
          (which the driver process inherits), a `Monitor`, loggers for `Http.Event` and `Exec.Event`,
          a `Tactic[WebDriver.Error]` and `Diagnostics`. The `.sessional` given constructs it, so a
          caller never names the class.
      entry #session   forks the driver, waits for it to listen, runs the block, and stops it
          Runs `lambda` with a fresh `WebDriver.Session` in scope, then aborts the driver's job in a
          `finally`. A failure to fork is reported as `WebDriver.Error` with reason
          `SessionNotCreated`; a driver that does not answer `GET /status` within five seconds as one
          with reason `Timeout`.
    entry .sessional   the given which lets `local.session: session ?=> …` resolve
        In the companion of `WebDriver.Local`, so it is found with no import whenever the contextual
        values it needs are in scope: `Online`, `Http.Backend`, `WorkingDirectory`, `Environment`,
        `Monitor`, `Http.Event is Loggable`, `Exec.Event is Loggable`, `Tactic[WebDriver.Error]` and
        `Diagnostics`.
    entry #driver   the driver program which drives this browser
    entry #port   the port the driver listens on; `4444` by default
    entry #arguments   the arguments the browser is launched with, such as `--headless=new`
    entry #requested   a capabilities object to send instead of the generated one, or `Unset`
    entry #on   the same browser with its driver on another port
        Each session on a port needs that port free, so sessions run concurrently each take their
        own: `WebDriver.Chrome.on(4451)`.
    entry #headless   the same browser launched without a window
        Appends the driver's headless arguments (`WebDriver.Driver#headless`) to `#arguments`; on
        Safari, which has no headless mode, it changes nothing.
    entry #arguing   the same browser with more launch arguments
        `WebDriver.Chrome.arguing(t"--window-size=1280,800", t"--lang=en")` appends the arguments
        the browser is started with, as `goog:chromeOptions`, `moz:firefoxOptions` or
        `ms:edgeOptions` says.
    entry #requesting   the same browser, with the capabilities object replaced wholesale
        For what `WebDriver.Local` does not model — proxies, timeouts, mobile emulation, a
        grid's extensions — the whole `POST /session` payload can be supplied as `Json`, replacing
        the generated `alwaysMatch` object.
    entry #capabilities   the `POST /session` payload: an `alwaysMatch` object naming the browser and its arguments
        The W3C shape, `{"capabilities": {"alwaysMatch": {…}}}`, with the browser's name and its
        vendor options object (`goog:chromeOptions`, `moz:firefoxOptions` or `ms:edgeOptions`,
        carrying `#arguments`; Safari takes only its name). Only `alwaysMatch` is generated, since
        `firstMatch` exists to let one request suit several drivers and a `Local` names exactly one.
        When `#requested` is set, it is returned instead.
  entry .Chrome   Chrome, through `chromedriver` on port 4444
      `WebDriver.Local(WebDriver.Driver.Chromedriver)`: `WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session: …` opens
      a headless Chrome session.
  entry .Firefox   Firefox, through `geckodriver` on port 4444
  entry .Safari   Safari, through `safaridriver` on port 4444
      Safari has no headless mode, so `WebDriver.Safari.headless` is the same as `WebDriver.Safari`.
  entry .Edge   Microsoft Edge, through `msedgedriver` on port 4444
  entry .Sessional   opens a session on a driver that is already listening
    detail
        The `aperture.Sessional` instance for `WebDriver`, with `Self = WebDriver` and `Result =
        Session^`. It creates the session with `POST /session` and the driver's `#capabilities`,
        runs the block with the handle in scope, and deletes the session — which is what closes the
        browser — in a `finally`, swallowing a failure to delete, since a driver that has already
        died cannot tidy up and saying so would hide the block's own error. Launching a local driver
        (`WebDriver.Local.Sessional`) is composition over this. It needs an `Online`, an
        `Http.Backend`, a logger for `Http.Event`, a `Tactic[WebDriver.Error]` and `Diagnostics`.
    entry #session   creates the session, runs the block with it in scope, and deletes it
        The session's `Result` type is a fresh `Session^`: each call's handle is its own capability,
        so the block cannot return it or anything capturing it.
  entry .sessional   the given which lets `webDriver.session: session ?=> …` resolve
      In the companion of `WebDriver`, so it is found with no import when an `Online`, an
      `Http.Backend`, an `Http.Event is Loggable`, a `Tactic[WebDriver.Error]` and `Diagnostics` are
      in scope.
  entry .apply   a driver already listening at a base URL, with the capabilities to request
      `WebDriver(url"http://localhost:4444", capabilities)` drives a driver started by hand, or a
      Selenium grid, through the same `Session` interface as a local browser. The capabilities are
      sent verbatim as the `POST /session` payload; `WebDriver.Chrome.capabilities` is a ready-made
      one, and `t"{}".read[Json]` is enough for a driver that needs none.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      WebDriver(url"http://localhost:4444", WebDriver.Firefox.headless.capabilities).session:
        browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
        browser.title()
      ```
  entry #base   the URL the driver listens at; commands are addressed beneath `base/session/id`
  entry #capabilities   the JSON sent as the `POST /session` payload
  entry .Error   a failed WebDriver action: the W3C error code, the driver's message and its stack trace
    detail
        Error 589: _the WebDriver action failed because `reason`: `detail`_, with the
        `WebDriver.Error.Reason`'s number as its subcode. Every failure of a session arrives as
        this one type: an error object the driver returned in a 4xx or 5xx reply, a driver that
        could not be reached, a reply which was not the JSON the specification describes (reason
        `UnknownError`), a reply cut short, a driver that could not be started (`SessionNotCreated`)
        or one which never began listening (`Timeout`). It is raised through the
        `Tactic[WebDriver.Error]` in scope when the session was opened.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")

            safely(browser.element(H2)).or:
              browser.element(H1)
        ```

        The driver's own message is `#detail`, and its stack trace, one line per element, is
        `#browserStacktrace`. `#data` keeps the specification's optional extra object, which is
        what carries the prompt's text on an `UnexpectedAlertOpen`: exactly what a caller needs to
        decide whether to accept or dismiss it.
    refer tarantula.WebDriver.Error.Reason
    entry .Reason   the W3C error code a driver reported, as an enumeration with stable numbers
      detail
          One case for every error code the WebDriver specification defines, with the code as the
          driver spells it in the `error` field of its error object:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  W3C code
          row  `NoSuchElement`  1  _the requested element was not found_  `no such element`
          row  `StaleElement`  2  _the element is no longer attached to the page_  `stale element reference`
          row  `NoSuchWindow`  3  _the requested window was not found_  `no such window`
          row  `JavascriptError`  4  _a JavaScript error occurred while evaluating it_  `javascript error`
          row  `ScriptTimeout`  5  _the asynchronous script did not complete in time_  `script timeout`
          row  `Timeout`  6  _the operation timed out_  `timeout`
          row  `UnknownCommand`  7  _the WebDriver does not recognise the command_  `unknown command`
          row  `Other(code)`  8  _the WebDriver reported the error code `code`_  any other, kept as `code`
          row  `ElementClickIntercepted`  9  _the click was intercepted by another element_  `element click intercepted`
          row  `ElementNotInteractable`  10  _the element cannot be interacted with_  `element not interactable`
          row  `InsecureCertificate`  11  _the page was served with an untrusted certificate_  `insecure certificate`
          row  `InvalidArgument`  12  _an argument to the command was not acceptable_  `invalid argument`
          row  `InvalidCookieDomain`  13  _the cookie's domain does not match the current page_  `invalid cookie domain`
          row  `InvalidElementState`  14  _the element is in a state which forbids the action_  `invalid element state`
          row  `InvalidSelector`  15  _the selector was not a valid one_  `invalid selector`
          row  `InvalidSessionId`  16  _the session is no longer active_  `invalid session id`
          row  `MoveTargetOutOfBounds`  17  _the pointer target lies outside the viewport_  `move target out of bounds`
          row  `NoSuchAlert`  18  _no alert is currently open_  `no such alert`
          row  `NoSuchCookie`  19  _the requested cookie was not found_  `no such cookie`
          row  `NoSuchFrame`  20  _the requested frame was not found_  `no such frame`
          row  `NoSuchShadowRoot`  21  _the element has no shadow root_  `no such shadow root`
          row  `DetachedShadowRoot`  22  _the shadow root is no longer attached to the page_  `detached shadow root`
          row  `SessionNotCreated`  23  _a new session could not be created_  `session not created`
          row  `UnableToSetCookie`  24  _the cookie could not be set_  `unable to set cookie`
          row  `UnableToCaptureScreen`  25  _the screenshot could not be taken_  `unable to capture screen`
          row  `UnexpectedAlertOpen`  26  _an unexpected alert is open_  `unexpected alert open`
          row  `UnknownError`  27  _the WebDriver encountered an unknown error_  `unknown error`
          row  `UnknownMethod`  28  _the command does not support the HTTP method used_  `unknown method`
          row  `UnsupportedOperation`  29  _the WebDriver does not support the operation_  `unsupported operation`
          ```

          The numbers are the error's subcode and are frozen: 1 to 8 keep the meanings they were
          first published with, which is why `Other` sits at 8 out of alphabetical order, and
          every code added since appends from 9. A code the enumeration does not know becomes
          `Other`, so decoding is total and the code a driver sent is never lost.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          supervise:
            WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
              capture[WebDriver.Error](browser.element(Name[DomId](t"absent"))).reason
                == WebDriver.Error.Reason.NoSuchElement
          ```
      entry .apply   decodes a W3C error code, such as `no such element`, to its `Reason`
          Total: an unrecognised code is `Other(code)`.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*

          WebDriver.Error.Reason(t"stale element reference") == WebDriver.Error.Reason.StaleElement
          ```
      entry .Other   an error code the enumeration does not name, kept as the driver sent it
        entry #code0   the code exactly as the driver spelled it
      entry #number   the reason's frozen subcode, from 1 to 29
      entry #code   the code as the specification spells it, such as `no such element`
    entry .communicable   renders each `Reason` as a sentence, such as _the requested element was not found_
        The `Communicable` given for `WebDriver.Error.Reason`, in its companion's scope, which is
        what fills the `reason` in the error's message.
    entry #reason   the W3C error code the driver reported, as a `WebDriver.Error.Reason`
    entry #detail   the driver's message, or the raw reply when it was not a W3C error object
    entry #browserStacktrace   the driver's stack trace, one line per element; empty when it sent none
    entry #data   the error object's optional `data` member, which carries an open prompt's text
        On an `UnexpectedAlertOpen`, `data.text` is the prompt's text, so a caller can read it and
        decide between `WebDriver.Session#acceptAlert` and `WebDriver.Session#dismissAlert`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            capture[WebDriver.Error](browser.title()).data.let(_.text.as[Text])
        ```
  entry .Element   a handle on a node of the page: a pure value, usable only with its session in scope
    detail
        The opaque reference a driver returned for a DOM node, meaningful only to the session which
        produced it. It is a pure value holding neither a capability nor its session, so a `List`
        of them can be built and passed around freely; every operation on one — `click`,
        `innerText`, `value`, `attribute`, `element` and the other extensions in the `tarantula`
        package — takes the `WebDriver.Session` as a contextual parameter, so an element is inert
        outside its session's block.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
            val menu: WebDriver.Element = browser.element(Name[DomId](t"menu"))
            val items: List[WebDriver.Element] = menu \ Li \ cls"item"
            items.map(_.innerText())
        ```

        A leaked element is a dangling id rather than a live handle. One used after its page has
        changed raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `StaleElement`.
    refer tarantula.WebDriver.Session
    refer tarantula.element
    entry .encodable   encodes an element in the wire form a script argument or pointer origin takes
        The W3C _web element identifier_ form, `{"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf": id}`, so
        an element can be passed in `WebDriver.Session#execute`'s arguments as `element.in[Json]`,
        where the script sees the live DOM node.
    entry .\   every match for a locator beneath an element, or beneath each of a list of elements
        `element \ Li` asks the driver for every descendant matching any `Focusable` locator — an
        HTML tag, a `cls"…"` class list, a `Name[DomId]`, a `SelectorList`, an `XPath` or link
        text — and returns them in document order, an empty list for none. The second form, on a
        `List[WebDriver.Element]`, selects from every element found so far, so selections chain:
        `element \ Name[DomId](t"menu") \ Li \ cls"item"`. The protocol has no batch find, so the
        chained form makes one request per element, and nothing is deduplicated. It needs the
        `WebDriver.Session` in scope.

        It is spelled `\` rather than `/` because symbolism's generic `/`, which `soundness`
        exports, would claim that name for every receiver. Both forms are in the companion of
        `WebDriver.Element`, so they need no import.
    entry #elementId   the driver's opaque id for the node
  entry .ShadowRoot   the open shadow root an element hosts, as a handle for finding elements within it
    detail
        Reached with `element.shadowRoot()`. The protocol addresses a shadow root separately from
        an element, and only finding is valid against one, so it is a separate pure handle: search
        it with `\` for every match or `element` for exactly one, with the `WebDriver.Session` in
        scope. Only *open* shadow roots are reachable; asking for a closed one raises
        `WebDriver.Error` with reason `NoSuchShadowRoot`, and using one whose host has left the
        page raises `DetachedShadowRoot`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            val root: WebDriver.ShadowRoot = browser.element(Name[DomId](t"widget")).shadowRoot()
            root.element(Button).click()
            (root \ Li).length
        ```
    entry .\   every match for a locator within a shadow root, in document order
        `root \ Button` returns every element in the shadow tree matching any `Focusable`
        locator, or an empty list. It needs the `WebDriver.Session` in scope, and lives in the
        companion of `WebDriver.ShadowRoot`, so it needs no import.
    entry #shadowId   the driver's opaque id for the shadow root
  entry .Session   a live WebDriver session: one browser, its windows and cookies, confined to its block
    detail
        The near end of a conversation with a driver, addressed beneath `base/session/id`. A
        session is never constructed directly: `session` on a `WebDriver` or a `WebDriver.Local`
        opens one, lends it to the block as a contextual value, and deletes it — closing the
        browser — when the block ends. Inside the block, `browser` names it.

        It is a capability, so capture checking confines it, and every closure over it, to the
        block which owns it: returning the session, or a lambda which uses it, is a compile error.
        What its commands return — `Text`, `Data`, `Json`, `WebDriver.Element`s, rectangles — is
        pure data, since every command is a complete round trip.

        A script is grown one stage at a time: open a session, navigate, find, act, and read the
        result back.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        frame
          label Open a session
          code
              supervise:
                WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
                  browser.title()
        frame
          label Navigate to a page
          code
              supervise:
                WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
                  browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/search")
                  browser.title()
        frame
          label Find a field and type into it
          code
              supervise:
                WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
                  browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/search")
                  val field = browser.element(Name[DomId](t"query"))
                  field.value(t"spiders")
                  field.property(t"value")
        frame
          label Submit, and wait for the results
          code
              import retryTenacities.exponentialTenTimesTenacity

              supervise:
                WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
                  browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/search")
                  browser.element(Name[DomId](t"query")).value(t"spiders")
                  browser.press(Keypress.Enter)
                  browser.awaitElements(cls"result").map(_.innerText())
        ##
        ```

        Every command raises `WebDriver.Error` through the `Tactic` in scope when the session was
        opened, and needs nothing further. The waits (`#awaitElement`, `#awaitElements`,
        `#awaitUntil`) additionally take a `Tenacity`, a `Monitor` and a `Tactic[Tenacity.Error]`
        at the call.
    refer tarantula.browser
    refer tarantula.WebDriver.Element
    refer tarantula.WebDriver.Error
    entry .Rect   the bounding box of an element or a window, in CSS pixels
      detail
          Returned by the `windowRect`, `maximize`, `minimize` and `fullscreen` commands of a
          `WebDriver.Session` and by the `rect` extension on an element, and taken by
          `windowRect(rect)` to move and resize the window.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          supervise:
            WebDriver.Chrome.session:
              browser.windowRect(WebDriver.Session.Rect(0.0, 0.0, 1280.0, 800.0))
              browser.windowRect().width
          ```
      entry #x   the left edge, from the viewport's or the screen's origin
      entry #y   the top edge, from the viewport's or the screen's origin
      entry #width   the width, in CSS pixels
      entry #height   the height, in CSS pixels
    entry .cm   a length in centimetres, as the `Quantity[Metres[1]]` a print's page and margins take
        `WebDriver.Session.cm(1.5)` is fifteen millimetres. Any length quantity will do in a
        `Page` or `Margin` — `21.0*Centi(Metre)`, `8.5*Inch` — so this is only a convenience.
    entry .Page   the paper size a print is laid out on; US Letter by default
      detail
          Lengths are `Quantity[Metres[1]]`, so they can be written in any unit, and are sent to
          the driver in the centimetres the protocol requires. The defaults are the
          specification's own: 21.59cm by 27.94cm.

          ```scala
          import soundness.*

          val a4 = WebDriver.Session.Page(WebDriver.Session.cm(21.0), WebDriver.Session.cm(29.7))
          ```
      entry #width   the page's width, as a length
      entry #height   the page's height, as a length
    entry .Margin   the four margins of a printed page; one centimetre each by default
      detail
          Lengths are `Quantity[Metres[1]]`, sent to the driver in centimetres. `.uniform` makes
          four equal margins.
      entry .uniform   four equal margins of the given length
          ```scala
          import soundness.*

          WebDriver.Session.Margin.uniform(WebDriver.Session.cm(0.5))
          ```
      entry #top   the top margin
      entry #bottom   the bottom margin
      entry #left   the left margin
      entry #right   the right margin
    entry .Orientation   whether a page is printed `Portrait` or `Landscape`
      entry #name   the orientation as the protocol spells it: `portrait` or `landscape`
    entry .Print   the options `WebDriver.Session#printPage` takes: orientation, scale, paper, margins, pages
      detail
          Every field has the specification's default: `Portrait`, a scale of `1.0`, no
          background graphics, a US Letter `Page`, one-centimetre `Margin`s, shrinking to fit, and
          every page. `pageRanges` is the protocol's own textual form, such as `List(t"1-3",
          t"5")`, since a range there may be open-ended.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          import WebDriver.Session.{Print, Orientation, Margin, cm}

          supervise:
            WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
              browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")

              browser.printPage:
                Print(orientation = Orientation.Landscape, margin = Margin.uniform(cm(0.5)))
          ```
      entry #orientation   `Portrait` or `Landscape`
      entry #scale   the scale factor, from 0.1 to 2.0; `1.0` by default
      entry #background   whether background colours and images are printed; `false` by default
      entry #page   the paper size, as a `WebDriver.Session.Page`
      entry #margin   the margins, as a `WebDriver.Session.Margin`
      entry #shrinkToFit   whether content is shrunk to fit the page width; `true` by default
      entry #pageRanges   the pages to print, as text such as `1-3` or `5`; every page when empty
    entry .Timeouts   the three timeouts a session keeps, in milliseconds
      detail
          `implicit` is how long a find command retries before reporting `NoSuchElement`, and is
          how a script waits for a page to settle without sleeping; `pageLoad` bounds navigation
          and `script` bounds `WebDriver.Session#execute` and `WebDriver.Session#executeAsync`. An `Unset` field is left as the
          driver has it.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          supervise:
            WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
              browser.timeouts(WebDriver.Session.Timeouts(`implicit` = 5000L))
              browser.timeouts().pageLoad
          ```

          The implicit timeout covers find commands and nothing else: a button becoming enabled
          or a heading's text changing is waited for with `WebDriver.Session#awaitUntil`.
      entry #implicit   how long a find retries before giving up, or `Unset` to leave it as it is
      entry #pageLoad   how long a navigation may take, or `Unset` to leave it as it is
      entry #script   how long a script may run, or `Unset` to leave it as it is
    entry .Action   one step of a keyboard or pointer input sequence, for `WebDriver.Session#perform`
      detail
          The W3C `/actions` endpoint is the only way to send a modifier, a chord, a hover or a
          drag, and an `Action` is one step of it: a key going down or up, a pause, a pointer
          button pressed or released, or a pointer movement. A list of steps is performed against
          one input source, a `WebDriver.Session.Action.Source`.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          import WebDriver.Session.Action

          supervise:
            WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
              val target = browser.element(Name[DomId](t"menu"))

              browser.perform
                ( Action.Source.Pointer,
                  List(Action.PointerMove(0, 0, origin = target), Action.Pause(500)) )
          ```

          Plain text entry into a field is simpler and faster with the `value` extension on an
          element, and keypresses are simplest with `WebDriver.Session#press`, which builds the
          steps with `.steps`.
      entry .Source   which input source a sequence of actions drives: `Key` or `Pointer`
        detail
            One `perform` call drives one source. A "none" source also exists in the
            specification, but it can only pause, which either of these can do; two sources in
            lockstep need the assembled JSON passed to `WebDriver.Session#actions`.
        entry #id   the source's id in the sequence: `keyboard` or `pointer`
        entry #kind   the source's type: `key` or `pointer`
      entry .LeftButton   the left mouse button, `0`, as the specification numbers buttons
      entry .MiddleButton   the middle mouse button, `1` in the DOM's numbering
      entry .RightButton   the right mouse button, `2` in the DOM's numbering, not `1` as one might guess
      entry .codepoint   the character a `Keypress` types, or the private-use codepoint for a named key
          A character key is itself; `Enter`, `Tab`, `Backspace`, `Escape`, the arrows, `Home`,
          `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`, `Insert`, `Delete` and `F1` to `F12` are the codepoints the
          specification assigns them in Unicode's private use area, from U+E000. A modifier on its
          own, a function key outside 1 to 12, or a terminal escape sequence is `Unset`.
      entry .steps   a `Keypress` as the key actions which produce it, modifiers held around it
        detail
            A modified keypress becomes its modifier pressed, the inner keypress struck, and the
            modifier released, nested to any depth, since the protocol has no command for "type
            this with control held". A keypress with no browser counterpart is no steps at all.

            ```scala fuel
            frame
              label A named key
              code
                  WebDriver.Session.Action.steps(Keypress.Enter)
                  // KeyDown(""), KeyUp("")
            frame
              label With Control held
              code
                  WebDriver.Session.Action.steps(Keypress.Ctrl('A'))
                  // KeyDown(""), KeyDown("A"), KeyUp("A"), KeyUp("")
            frame
              label With Control and Shift held
              code
                  WebDriver.Session.Action.steps(Keypress.Ctrl(Keypress.Shift(Keypress.Enter)))
                  // KeyDown(""), KeyDown(""), KeyDown(""),
                  // KeyUp(""), KeyUp(""), KeyUp("")
            ##
            ```
      entry .encodable   encodes an action as the specification's JSON, such as `{"type":"keyDown","value":"a"}`
          Written by hand, since the discriminator is a `type` field with camel-cased values and
          each variant carries its own keys; a `PointerMove` with no origin is relative to the
          `viewport`.
      entry .KeyDown   presses a key, by the character or codepoint it types
        entry #value   the key, as a character or a private-use codepoint from `WebDriver.Session.Action.codepoint`
      entry .KeyUp   releases a key, by the character or codepoint it types
        entry #value   the key, as a character or a private-use codepoint from `WebDriver.Session.Action.codepoint`
      entry .Pause   waits for a number of milliseconds before the next step
        entry #duration   the pause, in milliseconds
      entry .PointerDown   presses a pointer button; the left one by default
        entry #button   the button, numbered as the DOM does: `0` left, `1` middle, `2` right
      entry .PointerUp   releases a pointer button; the left one by default
        entry #button   the button, numbered as the DOM does: `0` left, `1` middle, `2` right
      entry .PointerMove   moves the pointer, relative to the viewport or to the centre of an element
        entry #x   the horizontal offset, in CSS pixels
        entry #y   the vertical offset, in CSS pixels
        entry #duration   how long the movement takes, in milliseconds; `100` by default
        entry #origin   the element whose centre the offsets are from, or `Unset` for the viewport
    entry #sessionId   the id the driver gave this session when it was created
    entry #capabilities   what the driver agreed to, which may differ from what was asked for
        The negotiated capabilities object from the driver's reply to `POST /session`: it names
        the browser and version which answered and says whether optional behaviour, such as
        `setWindowRect` or `acceptInsecureCerts`, is available. Vendor keys (`moz:*`, `goog:*`)
        are left as `Json`; `#browserName`, `#browserVersion` and `#platformName` read the common
        ones.
    entry #browserName   the name of the browser which answered, from the negotiated capabilities
    entry #browserVersion   the version of the browser which answered, or `Unset` if the driver did not say
    entry #platformName   the operating system the browser runs on, or `Unset` if the driver did not say
    entry #navigateTo   loads a URL in the current window, and waits for it to load
        Takes an `HttpUrl`, such as the result of `url"https://…"`, or any URL type which is
        `Abstractable across Urls to Text`. The overload for `HttpUrl` exists because the
        interpolator's result has the singleton type `Url["https"]`, which the generic form,
        declared for `Url["http" | "https"]`, would reject.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
            browser.url[HttpUrl]()
        ```
    entry #url   the current window's URL, as a URL type of the caller's choosing
        `browser.url[HttpUrl]()`, or any type `Instantiable across Urls from Text`.
    entry #back   goes back one page in the current window's history
    entry #forward   goes forward one page in the current window's history
    entry #refresh   reloads the current page
    entry #title   the current page's title
    entry #source   the current page's markup, as the browser serialises its DOM
        The DOM as it now stands, serialised, not the bytes the server sent: whatever the page's
        scripts have changed is included.
    entry #screenshotData   a PNG of the current viewport, or of one element, as raw bytes
        `screenshotData()` captures the viewport and `screenshotData(element)` one element. The
        bytes are those the driver sent, decoded from Base64. The `screenshot` extension in the
        `tarantula.image` component decodes them further, into a `Raster in Png`.
    entry #window   the handle of the current window or tab
    entry #windows   the handles of every open window and tab, in no particular order
    entry #closeWindow   closes the current window, returning the handles of those which remain
        Commands go to no window until `#switchTo` selects another; closing the last one ends the
        browser's session as far as the driver is concerned.
    entry #switchTo   makes another window or tab, by its handle, the target of every command
        Only the current window responds to commands, so a handle from `#newWindow` or `#windows`
        is passed here before the window can be used.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            val original = browser.window()
            val tab = browser.newWindow()
            browser.switchTo(tab)
            browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
            browser.closeWindow()
            browser.switchTo(original)
        ```
    entry #newWindow   opens a new tab, or a window, and returns its handle without switching to it
        `newWindow()` opens a tab and `newWindow(tab = false)` a window. As the specification
        says, the current window is left where it was: pass the handle to `#switchTo` to use it.
    entry #windowRect   the current window's position and size, or moves and resizes it
        `windowRect()` reads the window's `WebDriver.Session.Rect`; `windowRect(rect)` asks for a
        new one, which a window manager may not grant in full, and which a headless browser may
        ignore.
    entry #maximize   maximises the current window, returning the geometry it actually took
    entry #minimize   minimises the current window, returning the geometry it actually took
    entry #fullscreen   makes the current window full-screen, returning the geometry it actually took
    entry #frame   enters a frame, by its element or its index, as the target of every command
        Commands address the current browsing context, so entering a frame is a change of mode
        rather than an argument to each command. `#parentFrame` steps back out one level, and
        `#topFrame` returns to the top-level document from any depth. An absent frame raises
        `WebDriver.Error` with reason `NoSuchFrame`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.frame(browser.element(Iframe))
            browser.element(H1).innerText()
            browser.topFrame()
        ```
    entry #parentFrame   leaves the current frame for the one which contains it
    entry #topFrame   returns to the top-level document from a frame at any depth
        Sends an explicit `null` frame id, which the protocol distinguishes from an absent one.
    entry #acceptAlert   accepts the open alert, confirm or prompt, as clicking its OK button would
        While a prompt is open every other command fails with `WebDriver.Error` reason
        `UnexpectedAlertOpen`, whose `WebDriver.Error#data` carries the prompt's text; with no prompt open this
        raises `NoSuchAlert`.
    entry #dismissAlert   dismisses the open alert, confirm or prompt, as its Cancel button would
    entry #alertText   the open prompt's message, or types an answer into it
        `alertText()` reads the message of the open alert, confirm or prompt; `alertText(text)`
        types into a `prompt` before `#acceptAlert` submits it.
    entry #timeouts   the session's timeouts, or sets them
        `timeouts()` reads the `WebDriver.Session.Timeouts` in force; `timeouts(timeouts)` sets
        those which are not `Unset`, leaving the rest as they were.
    entry #cookies   every cookie visible to the current page
        Each a telekinesis `Cookie.Value`, whose fields are the names the protocol uses. It has
        no `sameSite`, so that attribute is neither read nor set.
    entry #cookie   the cookie of the given name visible to the current page
        Raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `NoSuchCookie` if there is none.
    entry #addCookie   sets a cookie on the current page's domain
        Takes a `Cookie.Value`. A cookie for another domain raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason
        `InvalidCookieDomain`, so navigate to the domain first. A cookie which needs a `sameSite`
        attribute can be set with `#execute` instead.
    entry #deleteCookie   deletes the cookie of the given name
    entry #deleteCookies   deletes every cookie visible to the current page
    entry #perform   performs a sequence of `WebDriver.Session.Action`s on one input source
        Sends the steps as a single sequence to the `/actions` endpoint, on the keyboard or the
        pointer source. Keys and buttons left held stay held until `#releaseActions`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import WebDriver.Session.Action

        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.perform
              ( Action.Source.Pointer,
                List(Action.PointerMove(10, 10), Action.PointerDown(), Action.PointerUp()) )
        ```
    entry #actions   sends an assembled `/actions` payload, for sources moving in lockstep
        The escape hatch beneath `#perform`, for the rare chord across keyboard and pointer: the
        `Json` is sent as the request body, `{"actions": […]}`, unchanged.
    entry #press   types a sequence of keypresses, with their modifiers held around them
        Each `Keypress` — a character, a named key such as `Keypress.Enter` or
        `Keypress.FunctionKey(3)`, or one wrapped in `Ctrl`, `Shift`, `Alt` or `Meta` — becomes
        the key actions of `WebDriver.Session.Action.steps`, all performed on the keyboard source
        in one request. Filling a field with plain text is better done with the `value`
        extension on an element: one request rather than several actions per character.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.element(Name[DomId](t"query")).click()
            browser.press(Keypress.Ctrl('A'), Keypress.Backspace, Keypress.Enter)
        ```
    entry #releaseActions   releases every key and button left held, and clears the input state
    entry #printPage   the current page printed as a PDF, in raw bytes
        Takes `WebDriver.Session.Print` options, the specification's defaults when none are given.
        This is what the page would print as, not what is rendered: `#screenshotData` captures
        that. Not every driver implements it — Safari does not — and one which does not raises
        `WebDriver.Error` with reason `UnknownCommand` or `UnsupportedOperation`.
    entry #execute   runs a JavaScript function body in the page and returns its result as `Json`
        The escape hatch for anything the protocol does not model. The script is a function body,
        so it uses `return`, and its arguments are `arguments[0]`, `arguments[1]` and so on: a
        `WebDriver.Element` passed as `element.in[Json]` arrives as its live DOM node, and an
        element returned comes back in the wire form. A script which throws raises
        `WebDriver.Error` with reason `JavascriptError`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            val heading = browser.element(H1)
            browser.execute(t"return arguments[0].offsetHeight", List(heading.in[Json])).as[Int]
        ```
    entry #executeAsync   runs asynchronous JavaScript, returning the value it passes to its callback
        As `#execute`, but the script is given a callback as its last argument and the command
        completes when the script calls it; one which does not within the `script` timeout raises
        `WebDriver.Error` with reason `ScriptTimeout`.
    entry #element   the one element a locator matches, at page scope or beneath an element or shadow root
        `browser.element(H1)` asks the driver for the first match for any `Focusable` locator: an
        HTML tag, a `cls"…"` class list, a `Name[DomId]`, a `SelectorList`, an `XPath`, or `Text`
        as link text. Nothing matching raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `NoSuchElement`, after
        the session's implicit timeout. The forms taking a `WebDriver.Element` or a
        `WebDriver.ShadowRoot` search beneath it, and are usually written through the `element`
        extension: `menu.element(Li)`.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.element(Name[DomId](t"greeting")).innerText()
            browser.element(unsafely(SelectorList.parse(t"nav li.active")))
        ```
    entry #elements   every element a locator matches, at page scope or beneath an element or shadow root
        As `#element`, but returns every match in document order, and an empty list rather than
        an error for none. `browser \ Li` is the same at page scope.
    entry #\   every element a locator matches at page scope, as `browser \ Li`
        `#elements` by another name. Selection is `\` rather than `/`, since symbolism's generic
        `/`, which `soundness` exports, claims the name for every receiver. On the result, `\`
        again selects beneath each element found: `browser \ Ul \ Li`.
    entry #activeElement   the element which has the focus
    entry #awaitElements   every match for a locator, polled until there is at least one
        The protocol has no wait command beyond the implicit timeout for finds, so this polls
        `POST /elements` under the `Tenacity` in scope, which is the caller's choice of schedule,
        and raises `Tenacity.Error` when the policy gives up. It also needs a `Monitor`. It
        polls the plural endpoint deliberately: that reports absence as an empty list, where the
        singular one raises an error that, being routed to the session's own tactic, could not be
        caught here to drive a retry.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import retryTenacities.exponentialTenTimesTenacity

        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.awaitElements(cls"result").length
        ```
    entry #awaitElement   the first match for a locator, polled until there is one
        As `#awaitElements`, returning the first element found; needs a `Tenacity`, a `Monitor`
        and a `Tactic[Tenacity.Error]`.
    entry #awaitUntil   polls a condition under the `Tenacity` in scope until it holds
        The general wait, for anything the caller can evaluate without raising: a title, a count
        of elements, an attribute, a cookie. The condition is re-evaluated at each attempt, and
        `Tenacity.Error` is raised when the policy gives up.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import retryTenacities.exponentialTenTimesTenacity

        supervise:
          WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
            browser.element(Button).click()
            browser.awaitUntil(browser.title() == t"Done")
        ```
    entry #shadowRoot   the open shadow root an element hosts
        Usually written through the `shadowRoot` extension on the element. A closed or absent
        shadow root raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `NoSuchShadowRoot`.
    entry #click   clicks an element at its centre, scrolling it into view first
        Usually written through the `click` extension: `button.click()`. An element covered by
        another raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `ElementClickIntercepted`, and one which
        cannot be clicked `ElementNotInteractable`.
    entry #clear   empties an editable element, such as an input or a text area
    entry #value   types text into an element, as the keyboard would
        Usually written through the `value` extension: `field.value(t"search terms")`. Text is
        appended to what is already there; `#clear` it first to replace it.
    entry #innerText   an element's rendered text, by the `innerText` algorithm
        What a reader sees, with hidden text omitted and line breaks where the layout puts them,
        rather than the DOM's `textContent`. Named `innerText` rather than `text`, which is
        already an extension in the `soundness` umbrella.
    entry #tagName   an element's tag name, such as `h1`
    entry #role   an element's computed ARIA role, such as `button` or `heading`
    entry #label   an element's computed accessible name, as a screen reader would announce it
    entry #enabled   whether a form control is enabled
    entry #selected   whether a checkbox, radio button or option is selected
    entry #attribute   an attribute as the markup has it, or `Unset` if the element has none
        Differs from `#property` as soon as a script touches the node: the `value` attribute of
        an input is what the page was served with, its property what the field holds now.
    entry #property   a live DOM property of an element, or `Unset` if it has none
    entry #css   the computed value of a CSS property on an element, such as `color`
    entry #rect   an element's bounding box, as a `WebDriver.Session.Rect` in CSS pixels
    entry #displayed   whether an element is displayed, by the specification's displayedness check
        The W3C specification dropped the JSON Wire Protocol's `displayed` command, so this runs
        the element's `getClientRects().length > 0` as a script, as every conforming client does.

entry .browser   the `WebDriver.Session` in scope, so that commands read as `browser.title()`
    Inside a `session` block the handle is a contextual value; `browser` names it, with its
    capture set kept as the session's own, so whatever it returns is confined to the same
    block. It is a method taking the session as a named contextual parameter, not a `summon`,
    which would mint a fresh capability rather than refer to the one the block owns.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
        browser.source().length
    ```

entry .click   clicks an element at its centre, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#click`. An element
    covered by another raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason `ElementClickIntercepted`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        browser.element(Button).click()
    ```

entry .clear   empties an editable element, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#clear`.

entry .innerText   an element's rendered text, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`: what a reader sees, by the `innerText` algorithm,
    rather than the DOM's `textContent`. It is not called `text`, since `text` is already an
    extension in the `soundness` umbrella.

entry .tagName   an element's tag name, such as `h1`, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#tagName`.

entry .enabled   whether a form control is enabled, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#enabled`.

entry .selected   whether a checkbox, radio button or option is selected
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#selected`; it needs
    the session in scope.

entry .displayed   whether an element is displayed, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`. The W3C protocol has no displayedness command, so
    `WebDriver.Session#displayed` evaluates one as a script.

entry .role   an element's computed ARIA role, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#role`.

entry .label   an element's computed accessible name, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#label`: the name a
    screen reader would announce, from its text, its `aria-label` or its associated `<label>`.

    The `soundness` umbrella also exports wisteria's `label`, the name of a field or case
    within a derivation, so under `import soundness.*` the two names collide and
    `element.label()` may not resolve; `browser.label(element)` reaches the same command
    without the extension.

entry .cssValue   the computed value of a CSS property on an element, such as `color`
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#css`; it needs the
    session in scope.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        browser.element(H1).cssValue(t"font-size")
    ```

entry .shadowRoot   the open shadow root an element hosts, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, returning a `WebDriver.ShadowRoot` to search with `\`
    or `element`. A closed or absent root raises `WebDriver.Error` with reason
    `NoSuchShadowRoot`.

entry .rect   an element's bounding box in CSS pixels, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, returning a `WebDriver.Session.Rect`.

entry .value   types text into an element, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, delegating to `WebDriver.Session#value`. The text is
    appended to what the field already holds, and is one request however long it is; named
    keys and chords need `WebDriver.Session#press`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        val field = browser.element(Name[DomId](t"query"))
        field.clear()
        field.value(t"search terms")
        field.property(t"value")
    ```

entry .screenshotData   a PNG of one element, as raw bytes, through the `WebDriver.Session` in scope
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`. The `screenshot` extension, in `tarantula.image`,
    decodes the same bytes into a `Raster in Png`.

entry .attribute   an element's attribute as the markup has it, or `Unset` if it has none
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, needing the session in scope. It differs from
    `property` once a script touches the node: an input's `value` attribute is what the page
    was served with, its `value` property what the field holds now.

entry .property   a live DOM property of an element, or `Unset` if it has none
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element`, needing the session in scope.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        val box = browser.element(Name[DomId](t"agree"))
        box.click()
        (box.attribute(t"checked"), box.property(t"checked"))
    ```

entry .element   the one element a locator matches beneath an element or a shadow root
    An extension on `WebDriver.Element` and on `WebDriver.ShadowRoot`, taking any `Focusable`
    locator and needing the session in scope: `menu.element(Li)`. Nothing matching raises
    `WebDriver.Error` with reason `NoSuchElement`; `\` returns every match instead, and an
    empty list for none.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        val form = browser.element(Form)
        form.element(Name[DomId](t"submit")).click()
    ```

entry .screenshot   a screenshot of the viewport or of one element, decoded as a `Raster in Png`
    An extension on `WebDriver.Session` and on `WebDriver.Element`, in the `tarantula.image`
    component, which keeps the image codec out of browser automation: `screenshotData`,
    decoded by hallucination's PNG codec. A screenshot the codec cannot read is thrown, as it
    would mean the driver sent something which was not a PNG.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    supervise:
      WebDriver.Chrome.headless.session:
        browser.navigateTo(url"https://example.com/")
        val page: Raster in Png = browser.screenshot()
        val heading: Raster in Png = browser.element(H1).screenshot()
    ```
